<!-- TODO (Sam): add a citation for the ML-NMR-B paper once it has a reference -->

Network meta-analysis (NMA) and multilevel network meta-regression (ML-NMR) require a *connected* network of treatments, so that every treatment can be compared through randomised evidence.
In practice networks are often *disconnected*: some treatments are only compared within a separate sub-network, or are only studied in single-arm studies.
This vignette demonstrates how to reconnect a disconnected network with ML-NMR, an approach we call ML-NMR with bridging (ML-NMR-B).

Bridging relies on the assumption of *conditional constancy of absolute effects*: after adjusting for the observed covariates, the absolute outcomes on a common treatment are exchangeable between the bridged study populations.
This assumption cannot be tested directly, so alongside the bridging methods we show how to assess its plausibility.
We demonstrate three ways of bridging:

* A [random baseline bridge](#random_bridge), which estimates a predictive distribution for the baseline of a new study from the reference sub-network (*baseline synthesis*), and uses it as an informative prior on the baselines of the disconnected studies.
* A [fixed baseline bridge](#fixed_bridge), which assumes that the adjusted baselines of two studies, one from each sub-network, are identical.
* An [external bridge](#external_bridge), which is a fixed baseline bridge using a study from outside the network, such as an observational cohort.

These methods are related to the approaches of @Thom2022 and @Beliveau2017, but adjust for differences between study populations at the individual level using ML-NMR.

We use the plaque psoriasis network of @Phillippo2022 (see `vignette("example_plaque_psoriasis")`), which is connected.
We deliberately disconnect it by removing arms, so that the results of each bridging method can be compared with those from the full, connected network as a gold standard.

# Setup
## Preparing the data
We transform the covariates in the IPD and AgD as in `vignette("example_plaque_psoriasis")`.
As well as the five effect modifiers used there, we also include age, sex, and baseline PASI score as prognostic factors.

```{r}
pso_ipd <- plaque_psoriasis_ipd %>%
  mutate(
    # Variable transformations
    bsa = bsa / 100,
    weight = weight / 10,
    durnpso = durnpso / 10,
    prevsys = as.numeric(prevsys),
    psa = as.numeric(psa),
    male = as.numeric(male),
    age = age / 10,
    pasi_w0 = pasi_w0 / 10,
    # Treatment classes
    trtclass = case_when(trtn == 1 ~ "Placebo",
                         trtn %in% c(2, 3, 5, 6) ~ "IL-17 blocker",
                         trtn == 4 ~ "TNFa blocker",
                         trtn == 7 ~ "IL-12/23 blocker"),
    # Check complete cases for covariates of interest
    is_complete = complete.cases(durnpso, prevsys, bsa, weight, psa)
  ) %>%
  filter(is_complete) %>%
  arrange(studyc, trtn)

pso_agd <- plaque_psoriasis_agd %>%
  mutate(
    # Variable transformations
    bsa_mean = bsa_mean / 100,
    bsa_sd = bsa_sd / 100,
    weight_mean = weight_mean / 10,
    weight_sd = weight_sd / 10,
    durnpso_mean = durnpso_mean / 10,
    durnpso_sd = durnpso_sd / 10,
    prevsys = prevsys / 100,
    psa = psa / 100,
    male = male / 100,
    age_mean = age_mean / 10,
    age_sd = age_sd / 10,
    pasi_w0_mean = pasi_w0_mean / 10,
    pasi_w0_sd = pasi_w0_sd / 10,
    # Treatment classes
    trtclass = case_when(trtn == 1 ~ "Placebo",
                         trtn %in% c(2, 3, 5, 6) ~ "IL-17 blocker",
                         trtn == 4 ~ "TNFa blocker",
                         trtn == 7 ~ "IL-12/23 blocker")
  ) %>%
  arrange(studyc, trtn)
```

The outcomes are ordered categorical (achieving PASI 75, PASI 90, and PASI 100).
We write a small helper to set up a network from IPD and AgD data frames, since we will create several networks.

```{r}
make_pso_net <- function(ipd, agd) {
  combine_network(
    set_ipd(ipd,
            study = studyc,
            trt = trtc,
            r = multi(r0 = 1,
                      PASI75 = pasi75,
                      PASI90 = pasi90,
                      PASI100 = pasi100,
                      type = "ordered", inclusive = TRUE),
            trt_class = trtclass),
    set_agd_arm(agd,
                study = studyc,
                trt = trtc,
                r = multi(r0 = pasi75_n,
                          PASI75 = pasi75_r,
                          PASI90 = pasi90_r,
                          PASI100 = pasi100_r,
                          type = "ordered", inclusive = TRUE),
                trt_class = trtclass),
    trt_ref = "PBO"
  )
}

add_pso_integration <- function(net) {
  add_integration(net,
    durnpso = distr(qgamma, mean = durnpso_mean, sd = durnpso_sd),
    prevsys = distr(qbern, prob = prevsys),
    bsa = distr(qlogitnorm, mean = bsa_mean, sd = bsa_sd),
    weight = distr(qgamma, mean = weight_mean, sd = weight_sd),
    psa = distr(qbern, prob = psa),
    age = distr(qgamma, mean = age_mean, sd = age_sd),
    male = distr(qbern, prob = male),
    pasi_w0 = distr(qgamma, mean = pasi_w0_mean, sd = pasi_w0_sd),
    n_int = 64)
}
```

## The full network
The full network is connected, and serves as the gold standard against which we compare the bridging methods.

```{r pso_full_net}
pso_net <- add_pso_integration(make_pso_net(pso_ipd, pso_agd))
pso_net
```

## Creating a disconnected network
We disconnect the network by removing the placebo arms of ERASURE, FEATURE, and JUNCTURE, and the secukinumab arms of FIXTURE, and dropping CLEAR.
This leaves two sub-networks:

* Sub-network 1, containing placebo, etanercept, ixekizumab, and ustekinumab (FIXTURE, IXORA-S, UNCOVER-1, UNCOVER-2, UNCOVER-3);
* Sub-network 2, containing only the two doses of secukinumab (ERASURE, FEATURE, JUNCTURE).

```{r}
pso_agd_disc <- pso_agd %>%
  filter(
    studyc != "CLEAR",
    !(studyc == "ERASURE" & trtc == "PBO"),
    !(studyc == "FEATURE" & trtc == "PBO"),
    !(studyc == "FIXTURE" & trtc %in% c("SEC_150", "SEC_300")),
    !(studyc == "JUNCTURE" & trtc == "PBO")
  )

pso_net_disc <- add_pso_integration(make_pso_net(pso_ipd, pso_agd_disc))
pso_net_disc
```

```{r pso_disc_net_plot, fig.width=8, fig.height=6}
plot(pso_net_disc, weight_nodes = TRUE, weight_edges = TRUE, show_trt_class = TRUE) +
  ggplot2::theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.box = "vertical")
```

Without bridging, the relative effects of secukinumab against the treatments in sub-network 1 cannot be estimated.

# Assessing population overlap
Bridging assumes that, after adjusting for the observed covariates, the bridged study populations are exchangeable.
Where two study populations overlap poorly on the observed covariates, the outcome model must extrapolate further, and it is more likely that the populations also differ on unobserved factors.
We assess the overlap between each pair of study populations with `compare_populations()`.
This fits a propensity score model to the full joint covariate distributions (using the integration points for AgD studies) and reports the *overlap effective sample size* (overlap ESS%) for each pair of studies; higher values indicate more similar populations.

```{r pso_overlap}
pso_overlap <- compare_populations(pso_net_disc,
                                   covariates = c("durnpso", "prevsys", "bsa", "weight", "psa",
                                                  "age", "male", "pasi_w0"),
                                   method = "propensity")
pso_overlap
```

```{r pso_overlap_plot, fig.width=8, fig.height=6}
plot(pso_overlap)
```

The three UNCOVER studies have high overlap with all of the studies in sub-network 2, and UNCOVER-3 with ERASURE is the most similar pair.
JUNCTURE and IXORA-S is the least similar pair.
These overlap measures guide which studies we choose to bridge in the following sections.

# Reference: full network ML-NMR {#full_network}
For comparison, we first fit the ML-NMR model to the full connected network.
As in `vignette("example_plaque_psoriasis")`, we include covariate-treatment interactions for the five effect modifiers with common interactions within each treatment class, and here also main effects for age, sex, and baseline PASI score.

```{r pso_fit_full}
pso_regression <- ~(durnpso + prevsys + bsa + weight + psa)*.trt + pasi_w0 + age + male

pso_fit_full <- nma(pso_net,
                    trt_effects = "fixed",
                    link = "probit",
                    regression = pso_regression,
                    class_interactions = "common",
                    prior_intercept = normal(scale = 10),
                    prior_trt = normal(scale = 10),
                    prior_reg = normal(scale = 10),
                    prior_aux = flat(),
                    QR = TRUE,
                    init_r = 0.2)
```

# Random baseline bridge {#random_bridge}
The random baseline bridge has two stages.

## Stage 1: Baseline synthesis
In the first stage, we use `baseline_synthesis()` to fit an ML-NMR model to the whole network with a random effect on the study baselines $\mu_j$ in the reference sub-network (sub-network 1):
$$\mu_j \sim \mathrm{N}(m_\mu, \tau_\mu^2).$$
The between-study standard deviation $\tau_\mu$ captures residual variation in the adjusted baselines due to unobserved differences between studies.
Studies in other sub-networks are included with independent baselines, so they inform the baseline synthesis only through the shared regression coefficients.
The random effect is placed on the network reference treatment, placebo, which is well informed in sub-network 1.

```{r pso_fit_stage1}
pso_fit_bs <- baseline_synthesis(pso_net_disc,
                                 trt_effects = "fixed",
                                 link = "probit",
                                 regression = pso_regression,
                                 class_interactions = "common",
                                 prior_intercept = normal(scale = 10),
                                 prior_intercept_sd = half_normal(scale = 5),
                                 prior_trt = normal(scale = 10),
                                 prior_reg = normal(scale = 10),
                                 prior_aux = flat(),
                                 QR = TRUE,
                                 init_r = 0.2)
pso_fit_bs
```

The output shows the posterior for the mean study baseline `baseline_mean` ($m_\mu$), the between-study SD `baseline_sd` ($\tau_\mu$), and the predictive distribution for the baseline in a new study `baseline_new` ($\mu_\mathrm{new}$).
All are on the latent probit scale, at the reference level of the covariates.

The predictive distribution $\mu_\mathrm{new} \mid m_\mu, \tau_\mu \sim \mathrm{N}(m_\mu, \tau_\mu^2)$ is normal only conditional on $m_\mu$ and $\tau_\mu$.
The posterior predictive distribution also includes the uncertainty in these parameters, which gives it heavier tails than a normal distribution, particularly when few studies inform the baseline synthesis.
`baseline_synthesis()` therefore approximates the posterior draws of `baseline_new` with a Student t distribution, fitted by maximum likelihood, and prints it at the end of the output.
This is stored in the fitted model as `baseline_prior`, ready to use as a prior in Stage 2:

```{r}
pso_fit_bs$baseline_prior
```

### Assessing heterogeneity in baseline risk
The size of $\tau_\mu$ indicates how much unexplained variation there is between the adjusted study baselines, and should be judged relative to the treatment effects being estimated.
A small $\tau_\mu$ supports the exchangeability of the study baselines.
Comparing the prior and posterior distributions shows whether $\tau_\mu$ is informed by the data, or is only weakly identified (with a posterior close to the prior).

```{r pso_bs_tau_prior_post}
plot_prior_posterior(pso_fit_bs, prior = "intercept_sd")
```

<!-- TODO (Sam): check against the plot once precompiled. -->

Here the posterior has concentrated well below the $\textrm{half-N}(0, 5^2)$ prior, so $\tau_\mu$ is both small and well estimated from the data.

## Stage 2: ML-NMR with predictive prior distributions
In the second stage we fit a standard ML-NMR model to the disconnected network, with independent baselines for every study.
The disconnected studies in sub-network 2 receive the Stage 1 predictive distribution as an informative prior on their baselines, which reconnects the network.
This is specified with the `connect_baseline` argument and `con(type = "random")`, giving the studies to receive the predictive prior.

Which studies to apply the predictive prior to depends on how plausible the conditional constancy of absolute effects assumption is judged to be for each.
Here all three studies in sub-network 2 have high overlap with sub-network 1, so we apply it to all three.

```{r pso_fit_random_3}
pso_fit_random_3 <- nma(pso_net_disc,
                        trt_effects = "fixed",
                        link = "probit",
                        regression = pso_regression,
                        class_interactions = "common",
                        connect_baseline = con(type = "random",
                                               studies = c("ERASURE", "FEATURE", "JUNCTURE"),
                                               prior_baseline = pso_fit_bs$baseline_prior),
                        prior_intercept = normal(scale = 10),
                        prior_trt = normal(scale = 10),
                        prior_reg = normal(scale = 10),
                        prior_aux = flat(),
                        init_r = 0.2)
```

For comparison, we also apply the predictive prior to a single anchor study, ERASURE, which has the greatest overlap with sub-network 1.

```{r pso_fit_random_1}
pso_fit_random_1 <- nma(pso_net_disc,
                        trt_effects = "fixed",
                        link = "probit",
                        regression = pso_regression,
                        class_interactions = "common",
                        connect_baseline = con(type = "random",
                                               studies = "ERASURE",
                                               prior_baseline = pso_fit_bs$baseline_prior),
                        prior_intercept = normal(scale = 10),
                        prior_trt = normal(scale = 10),
                        prior_reg = normal(scale = 10),
                        prior_aux = flat(),
                        init_r = 0.2)
```

# Fixed baseline bridge {#fixed_bridge}
A fixed baseline bridge assumes that the adjusted baselines of two studies, one from each sub-network, are identical.
This is equivalent to treating the two studies as arms of a single study.
We use `con(type = "fixed")`, naming the two studies to bridge.
We choose UNCOVER-3 and ERASURE, the pair with the greatest overlap ESS%.

```{r pso_fit_fixed}
pso_fit_fixed <- nma(pso_net_disc,
                     trt_effects = "fixed",
                     link = "probit",
                     regression = pso_regression,
                     class_interactions = "common",
                     connect_baseline = con(type = "fixed",
                                            studies = c("UNCOVER-3", "ERASURE")),
                     prior_intercept = normal(scale = 10),
                     prior_trt = normal(scale = 10),
                     prior_reg = normal(scale = 10),
                     prior_aux = flat(),
                     QR = TRUE,
                     init_r = 0.2)
```

Unlike the random baseline bridge, the fixed baseline bridge carries no additional uncertainty for between-study variation in the baselines, so it allows for no unobserved differences between the two bridged studies.

# External bridge {#external_bridge}
Bridging studies can also come from outside the network.
Here we add the PROSPECT cohort study [@Thaci2019], a single-arm study of secukinumab 300 mg, and bridge it to IXORA-S in sub-network 1 with a fixed baseline bridge.
The PROSPECT outcomes and covariate summaries are entered on the same scales as the other AgD studies.

```{r}
prospect <- tibble(
  studyc = "PROSPECT", trtc_long = "Secukinumab 300 mg", trtc = "SEC_300", trtn = 6,
  trtclass = "IL-17 blocker",
  pasi75_r = 1156, pasi75_n = 1509, pasi90_r = 801, pasi90_n = 1509, pasi100_r = 337, pasi100_n = 1509,
  bsa_mean = 0.187, bsa_sd = 0.184,
  weight_mean = 8.75, weight_sd = 2.03,
  durnpso_mean = 1.96, durnpso_sd = 1.35,
  prevsys = 0.9095, psa = 0.202,
  age_mean = 4.81, age_sd = 1.37,
  male = 0.624,
  pasi_w0_mean = 1.77, pasi_w0_sd = 1.25
)

pso_net_ext <- add_pso_integration(make_pso_net(pso_ipd, bind_rows(pso_agd_disc, prospect)))
```

We check the overlap between PROSPECT and the studies in sub-network 1, to choose the study to bridge it with.

```{r}
pso_overlap_ext <- compare_populations(pso_net_ext,
                                       covariates = c("durnpso", "prevsys", "bsa", "weight", "psa",
                                                      "age", "male", "pasi_w0"),
                                       method = "propensity")
pso_overlap_ext
```

PROSPECT has its greatest overlap with IXORA-S, so we bridge these two studies.

```{r pso_fit_external}
pso_fit_ext <- nma(pso_net_ext,
                   trt_effects = "fixed",
                   link = "probit",
                   regression = pso_regression,
                   class_interactions = "common",
                   connect_baseline = con(type = "fixed",
                                          studies = c("PROSPECT", "IXORA-S")),
                   prior_intercept = normal(scale = 10),
                   prior_trt = normal(scale = 10),
                   prior_reg = normal(scale = 10),
                   prior_aux = flat(),
                   QR = TRUE,
                   init_r = 0.2)
```

# Comparing the bridging methods
We now compare the population-average relative effects against placebo from each bridging method with those from the full connected network.
We focus on two study populations: FEATURE (a disconnected study in sub-network 2) and FIXTURE (a connected study in sub-network 1).
The relative effects of etanercept, ixekizumab, and ustekinumab are estimated within sub-network 1, whereas those of secukinumab rely on the bridge.

```{r}
pso_fits <- list("Full network" = pso_fit_full,
                 "Random (3 studies)" = pso_fit_random_3,
                 "Random (1 study)" = pso_fit_random_1,
                 "Fixed (UNCOVER-3/ERASURE)" = pso_fit_fixed,
                 "External (PROSPECT/IXORA-S)" = pso_fit_ext)

pso_releff <- bind_rows(lapply(pso_fits, function(f) {
  as_tibble(relative_effects(f))
}), .id = "Bridge") %>%
  filter(.study %in% c("FEATURE", "FIXTURE")) %>%
  mutate(Bridge = factor(Bridge, levels = rev(names(pso_fits))))
```

```{r pso_releff_compare, fig.width=8, fig.height=6}
ggplot(pso_releff, aes(x = mean, xmin = `2.5%`, xmax = `97.5%`, y = Bridge, colour = Bridge)) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, colour = "grey60") +
  geom_pointrange(size = 0.3) +
  facet_grid(.trtb ~ .study) +
  labs(x = "Relative effect vs. placebo (probit scale)", y = NULL) +
  theme_multinma() +
  theme(legend.position = "none", strip.text.y = element_text(angle = 0))
```

<!-- TODO (Sam): check this interpretation against the plot once precompiled. It follows the Chapter 3 paper results (marginal effects), whereas this plot shows conditional relative effects. -->

For etanercept, ixekizumab, and ustekinumab, all methods agree with the full network, since these comparisons do not depend on the bridge.
For secukinumab, the estimates depend on the bridging method:

* The random baseline bridge applied to all three disconnected studies gives estimates closest to the full network.
  Applying the predictive prior to a single study gives similar point estimates but wider credible intervals, because fewer study baselines are assumed exchangeable and so fewer studies contribute evidence to the bridge.
* The fixed baseline bridge between UNCOVER-3 and ERASURE gives narrow credible intervals, but estimates further from the full network, despite this pair having the greatest observed overlap.
  This suggests that UNCOVER-3 and ERASURE differ on unobserved factors, which the fixed bridge cannot allow for.
* The external bridge with PROSPECT differs more again from the full network.

High observed overlap does not guarantee that the conditional constancy of absolute effects assumption holds, since this depends on unobserved as well as observed differences.
We therefore recommend the random baseline bridge where the data allow, since it allows for unobserved differences between studies, and recommend sensitivity analyses across a range of plausible bridging choices.

